<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Shu</h1><p class="st-meta">405 CE – 413 CE · capital Chengdu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A brief breakaway state in Sichuan, ruled by Qiao Zong after a mutiny of 405, which the Eastern Jin reconquered in 413.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 405 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, unwilling to campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-xuan">Huan Xuan's</a> surviving relatives, mutinied and killed their commander Mao Qu. They chose the officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiao-zong">Qiao Zong</a> as leader, and he proclaimed himself Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Western Shu held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> plain and sought protection by accepting the overlordship of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a>, which recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiao-zong">Qiao Zong</a> as King of Shu. It also sheltered Huan clan fugitives and joined attacks on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> territory along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 412 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Liu Yu</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-lingshi">Zhu Lingshi</a>, who advanced by an unexpected route and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in 413. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiao-zong">Qiao Zong</a> fled and hanged himself, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> returned to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 405 CE – 413 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.76%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>405 CE – 413 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chengdu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qiao Zong</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shu (Qiao Zong)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=409&amp;focus=western-shu" aria-label="Western Shu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7376 1059 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Western Shu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7696 1315L7769 1191M7696 1315L7855 1179"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7696 1315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chengdu. <a href="/atlas?year=409&amp;focus=western-shu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

